// §0 Metadata. ✅ REWRITTEN against the September 2026 copy-rewrite brief
// (workbook row 25, "Commercial cleaning (hub)"), which scores this page
// against a 5,940/mo mapped cluster led by `commercial cleaning` (3,600/mo).
// Every volume in that brief comes from the Keyword Mapping & Metadata
// workbook, which carries no pull date; no live keyword, position or Search
// Console data was available, so none is quoted here either.
//
// TITLE is the workbook's approved line verbatim, at 55 characters. It adds
// "Contracts", which is the only place on the page that reaches
// `commercial contract cleaning services` (100/mo) in a heading or the head.
//
// ⚠️ WORKBOOK CHANGE REQUEST, raised and deliberately NOT acted on. The brief
// flags that "Contracts" pulls against this page's own strongest selling
// point: BFD §8.3 names being locked into a contract as one of four things
// this audience actively worries about, and the comparison table's
// "Commitment" row and branded FAQ 5 both answer it with "No lock-in, no
// minimum term, no exit fee". A searcher who clicks a result promising
// contracts and lands on a page selling the absence of one has been mis-sold,
// even favourably. The brief's suggested replacement for row 25 is
// "Commercial Cleaning Services, No Lock-In | TLB Cleaning" (55 characters).
// The approved line ships until row 25 is updated — that is the brief's own
// instruction, and it is the opposite call to the description below because a
// title that oversells is a marketing problem, not a credentials one.
//
// 🔴 DESCRIPTION IS *NOT* THE WORKBOOK'S APPROVED LINE. The approved meta reads:
//
//   "Commercial cleaning on contract, after hours, by the same police-checked
//    team each week. Offices, clinics, schools and more. Book a walk-through."
//
// The brief opens a blocking change request against it on two counts and
// supplies the replacement used below. Both are taken, on the same ruling that
// took the real estate page's approved meta out of service:
//
//   1. "POLICE-CHECKED" is a credential BFD §2.4 lists as unconfirmed, with
//      the explicit instruction "Police checked and insured appears in
//      supplied meta descriptions. Confirm both before publishing." This is a
//      claim about who enters a customer's premises after hours, published in
//      a search result, which is the least correctable place a wrong claim can
//      sit — and a meta description is the one surface where this codebase's
//      visible-bracket convention cannot be used. The same credential is
//      bracketed in body copy across four sibling pages; one answer fixes all
//      of them and this line goes back the day §2.4 confirms it.
//   2. "ON CONTRACT" is the title-tag conflict again, and sharper here,
//      because the meta is what the searcher reads before deciding to click.
//
// The replacement is the brief's own, verbatim, at 135 characters. It keeps
// the exact primary keyword in first position, keeps the approved CTA as its
// closing sentence, and states the no-lock-in position the page actually
// argues. The old live meta led with neither: it ran 174 characters, 24 over
// the limit, and split the primary across "commercial and office cleaning" so
// the exact phrase never appeared.
const pageMeta = {
  title: 'Commercial Cleaning Services - Contracts | TLB Cleaning',
  description:
    'Commercial cleaning after hours, by the same local team each week. Offices, clinics, schools and more. No lock-in. Book a walk-through.',
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/commercial-cleaning/',
};

const business = {
  name: 'TLB Cleaning',
  url: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/',
  logo: '',
  telephone: '',
  email: '',
  address: {
    streetAddress: '',
    addressLocality: '',
    addressRegion: '',
    postalCode: '',
    addressCountry: 'AU',
  },
  sameAs: [],
  // ✅ WIDENED with §14's town list, which the copy-rewrite brief restores to
  // all three regions per BFD §2.1. Structured data that claims NSW while the
  // page lists nine Queensland towns is a machine-readable contradiction, so
  // the two move together — same call, and the same note, as
  // /house-cleaning/ndis-cleaning/.
  //
  // ⚠️ STILL OPEN, and it is the brief's first client question: does TLB take
  // COMMERCIAL work in the Southern Gold Coast? The H1 and hero deliberately
  // still name two regions pending that answer (see §1), and the last
  // non-branded FAQ is still silent on QLD's Blue Card. If the answer is no,
  // this string, §14's `completeFootprintLocationGroups` and
  // `footprintSubheading` all revert together.
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers and Tweed NSW, and the Southern Gold Coast QLD',
  description: pageMeta.description,
};

// §1 Hero — drafted in full. Lead is 23 words, inside the 15-25 target.
//
// ⚠️ KICKER ADDED, not in the brief. The H1 contains no part of the focus
// keyword — neither "commercial", "office" nor "cleaning" appears in it —
// while the brief's own template row asks the headline to "overlap with key
// topic and audience targeting". Someone arriving from a SERP gets no
// confirmation they landed on the right page. Hero's `kicker` renders above
// the <h1>, outside it, so this orients the reader without editing approved
// copy. (Second consumer of the prop after why-tlb.)
//
// ⚠️ CTA LABEL CHANGED from the brief's "Get an instant quote". There is no
// instant quote for commercial work and the page says so twice: branded FAQ
// 5 promises a site walkthrough then a written scope and price, and the
// first non-branded FAQ closes "a site walkthrough settles it in ten
// minutes". The sitewide header CTA is untouched; this is page-level only.
//
// Hero style matches the homepage, house cleaning and deep cleaning pages:
// `split-single-image`, copy left, image right. The dashed pre-launch
// placeholder is GONE.
//
// A SCENE PHOTO, not a cut-out figure — the deliberate break from the
// sibling service pages, at TLB's direction. A studio cut-out on a plain
// background says "a cleaner"; it does not say commercial. This frame does:
// an office foyer, a boardroom behind glass, lobby seating and a commercial
// cleaning trolley, which is the single clearest signal that this is
// contract work rather than someone's house.
//
// So this hero is family A in IMAGE-GUIDELINES terms, which changes two
// things from the cut-out it replaced:
//   - `imageFit` is UNSET, taking Placeholder's `cover` default. There is no
//     transparency to preserve and no figure to stand on the section edge.
//   - `ratio` is 1/1 because the SOURCE is 1024x1024. Placeholder puts
//     `aspect-ratio` on the wrapper, so a box matching the file crops
//     nothing. Regenerate square if this is ever reshot, or change both
//     together — a 4/3 box over a square file would cover-crop the trolley
//     out of the left edge, and the trolley is why the frame works.
//
// Any leftover height under the box is --color-surface, the same Off White
// the section already paints, so it reads as the band rather than as a gap.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. An AI image of a person who does not
// exist, in the §3 canonical uniform (aqua polo, dark teal apron, teal
// gloves). Honest as generic service imagery, and NOT presented as a named
// team member, which IMAGE-GUIDELINES §7 forbids. A real photo of a real TLB
// cleaner on a real commercial site would still do more for a B2B buyer than
// anything on this page, so treat this as a good placeholder, not the
// finished job.
//
// ⚠️ Do NOT fill it from src/assets/hero/* — those are Maple template
// leftovers, not TLB's people.
// ✅ H1, LEAD AND CTA REWRITTEN (copy-rewrite brief §4, §6 and §7).
//
// THE H1 NO LONGER CARRIES ANY PART OF THE MAPPED PHRASE — that was the
// finding. `commercial cleaning` (3,600/mo) appeared nowhere in it, and the
// kicker below was added in the original build precisely because the heading
// gave a searcher no confirmation they had landed on the right page. A kicker
// is a patch on that problem; the H1 is the fix.
//
// The new H1 carries the primary contiguously, carries `commercial cleaning
// services` (1,400/mo, the largest mapped secondary) in the same breath, and
// the region does the work for `commercial cleaning services near me`
// (300/mo).
//
// THE OLD HEADLINE IS NOT CUT. It moves one line down into `subheading`,
// which is what that prop was added to Hero.astro for on the Airbnb, real
// estate and NDIS pages. "Cleaning should not be one of your jobs" is the
// cleanest statement of the BFD's core promise anywhere on this site and it
// still lands before anyone has scrolled.
//
// The brief's developer note says `split-single-reverse` does not render
// `.hero__subheading`. That was true when the brief was written and is not
// true now: Hero.astro wires the slot into all five variants, this one
// included. No component change was needed.
//
// ⚠️ IF THE SOUTHERN GOLD COAST ANSWER COMES BACK YES, the brief supplies the
// alternative H1 and nothing else changes:
//   'Commercial cleaning services across the Northern Rivers, the Tweed and the Southern Gold Coast'
//
// THE LEAD gains the exact phrase contiguously (it was split as "office and
// commercial cleaning"), drops the region now that the H1 owns it, and names
// the premises types the meta promises the searcher. "Easy to book, fast
// quoting, cost effective" is the correct BFD §13.2 variant for
// condition-priced work and is untouched.
//
// THE CTA is the workbook's approved label. The page had been spelling it two
// ways at once, "Book a site walkthrough" here and in the closing block
// against "Book a walkthrough" in the shared line copy, and neither matched
// the approved text a searcher clicked. One label, repeated, per BFD §9.5.
const hero = {
  kicker: 'Commercial cleaning',
  headingLines: ['Commercial cleaning services across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed'],
  subheading: 'Cleaning should not be one of your jobs.',
  lead: `Ongoing commercial cleaning for offices, clinics, schools and shopfronts, on a schedule that works around how you trade. We agree the scope in writing, then you stop thinking about it. ${theLine.fast}`,
  cta: { label: 'Book a walk-through', href: quoteCta.href },
  image: {
    ratio: '1/1',
    label:
      'TLB Cleaning cleaner mopping the polished floor of a bright office foyer, with a commercial cleaning trolley beside her and a boardroom behind glass',
    src: heroImg,
  },
};

// §2 Definition paragraph — the brief supplied NOTHING here (the row held
// only "CTA - Get an instant quote"), but this is the answer-target block
// for the focus keyword, and with the H1 carrying none of that keyword it is
// the first place on the page the phrase can appear in body copy.
//
// ⚠️ DRAFTED, NOT SUPPLIED — needs sign-off. Unlike the NDIS page's
// equivalent gap, nothing here is regulated: this is ordinary marketing
// copy, not a statement about a funded support. Sentence one describes the
// service and names the premises types the mega-menu already commits to;
// sentence two is the standard sibling pattern (what TLB does, where).
const definitionBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Commercial cleaning is the ongoing cleaning of a workplace, done to an agreed scope on a set schedule, usually before you open or after you close. Offices, clinics, venues, strata common areas, retail floors and kitchens. TLB Cleaning provides commercial cleaning services across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed.',
  'Commercial cleaning, contract cleaning and office cleaning are three names for much the same job. What changes between them is the kind of workplace and how often we come, not how the work is agreed or how it gets checked. This page covers all of it.',
];

// §3 Pathway cards — REWRITTEN. This was the brief's biggest defect.
//
// The three cards were lifted from ndis-cleaning.md and only partly adapted.
// Card 2 was not adapted at all: "We work directly with self-managed and
// plan-managed participants, sending clear, compliant invoices straight to
// your plan manager". Those are NDIS terms. They mean nothing to an office
// manager, and to anyone who recognises them they advertise that this page
// was assembled from another one. It could not ship in any form.
//
// Cards 1 and 3 were contaminated more mildly but still wrong in register —
// "regular support" is disability-sector language where the commercial word
// is service or contract; "who is at the door" describes a house, not a
// workplace with a back entrance and an alarm code; "warmth" is the right
// note for a home and the wrong one for a tenancy.
//
// All three are re-pointed at the three questions a commercial buyer
// actually arrives with, using answers this brief already contains
// elsewhere. Card 3 is near-verbatim from branded FAQ 2, which answered the
// question better than the original card did.
//
// The source titles each ended in a literal "→"; stripped, because
// PathwayCards renders its own CTA affordance and the arrow would duplicate
// it. (Same call as every sibling page.)
const pathwayCards = {
  // Not in the brief — placeholder structural copy.
  heading: 'People arrive here with three questions.',
  headingAccent: 'Which one is yours?',
  cards: [
